WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.900
How are user threads mapped

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.560
to kernel threads? The user-level

3
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.720
threading abstraction and the kernel

4
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.820
scheduling abstraction need not be

5
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.940
one to one. Follow four

6
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.520
user threads through the three

7
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.280
mappings, and keep the number

8
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.260
of physical processor cores visible.

9
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.920
In many to one, several

10
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.940
user threads share one kernel

11
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.780
thread. A user-level library selects

12
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.380
which logical thread uses that

13
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.920
kernel execution context. Since there

14
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.440
is only one such context

15
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:31.240
for these threads, they cannot

16
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.620
run simultaneously on two cores

17
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:34.440
through that mapping.

18
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.660
Suppose one of those user

19
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.260
threads makes a blocking kernel

20
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.260
call. If its sole kernel

21
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.160
thread blocks, the kernel has

22
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.480
no other runnable execution context

23
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.000
for the remaining user threads

24
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.520
in that group. They may

25
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.340
all lose progress even though

26
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.820
another user thread has work

27
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:51.240
to do.

28
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.340
Do not confuse that case

29
00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.740
with a library-level wait. A

30
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.560
cooperative library can park one

31
00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.080
logical thread and switch to

32
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.640
another without asking the kernel

33
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.100
to block the sole kernel

34
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.140
thread. Which layer performs the

35
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.840
wait determines whether the whole

36
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:06.740
group is stopped.

37
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:08.980
In one to one, each

38
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:10.680
user thread has a corresponding

39
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.300
kernel thread. The operating system

40
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.880
can schedule those kernel threads

41
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.800
independently, including on separate cores.

42
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.280
One blocked kernel thread therefore

43
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.240
does not inherently block every

44
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.460
peer thread in the process.

45
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.580
The cost is that creating

46
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.980
many logical threads also creates

47
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.600
many kernel-managed execution contexts. Their

48
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.240
stacks and management data consume

49
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.780
resources, and creation or switching

50
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.340
can cost more than a

51
00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.580
lightweight library-only operation. Greater scheduling

52
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:42.780
freedom comes with implementation costs.

53
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.980
Many to many maps a

54
00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.400
larger set of logical threads

55
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.720
onto a pool of kernel

56
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.220
threads. Here four user threads

57
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.600
share two kernel contexts, allowing

58
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.980
at most two of these

59
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.440
threads to execute at once

60
00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.000
when two cores are available.

61
00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.800
The runtime chooses the user-to-kernel

62
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.040
assignments over time.

63
00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.840
If one kernel context blocks,

64
00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.840
another may continue to run

65
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.360
logical work; the exact result

66
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.980
depends on the runtime design

67
00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:12.520
and available contexts. The mapping

68
00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.720
itself does not guarantee ideal

69
00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.680
load balance. A language feature

70
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.200
such as a coroutine also

71
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.460
does not, by itself, specify

72
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.600
one universal kernel-thread mapping.

73
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.400
The slides ask why not

74
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.460
map one sequential user thread

75
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.960
to many simultaneous kernel executions.

76
00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.540
One instruction stream cannot simply

77
00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.520
be executed twice as independent

78
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.660
progress without changing its semantics.

79
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.600
Extra execution contexts are useful

80
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.280
when there are multiple schedulable

81
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.960
logical activities to run.

82
00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.740
Things to take note of.

83
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.420
Count logical threads, kernel contexts

84
00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.820
and physical cores separately. For

85
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.800
every blocking question, state whether

86
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.320
the library parks a logical

87
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.080
thread or the kernel blocks

88
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.760
an execution context, then show

89
00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.700
which remaining path can actually

90
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:00.020
run.
